Letters
=======

A word processor for Microsoft Word documents (.docx), written in
JavaScript on the disc. It is being built: this version shows Word
documents as pages (with headers, footers, fields, columns, line
numbers and text flowing round floating pictures and tables), lets
you type, delete and format text (with undo), set up the pages, and
saves them: Save, Save as and Save a copy.

Opening a document
------------------
Click Letters' icon on the icon bar (or choose New on its menu or a
document's menu) for a new, empty document, Untitled (then Untitled
2...: saved as Untitled2, a file name has no spaces), ready to type
into; a double-click makes one. Recent, on the icon bar menu, lists the
last 8 documents opened or saved (kept in Choices:Letters): choose one
to open it. Double-click a .docx file in a directory display, or drop
one on Letters' icon on the icon bar (or on a document's window). Only
one Letters runs: more documents open in the same one, each in a window
of its own. Opening a document that is already open brings its window
to the front.

The window shows the text of the document in the fonts
it asks for (or ones with the same widths: Carlito for Calibri,
Caladea for Cambria, Liberation for Arial, Times New Roman and
Courier New). Bold, italic, underlining, colours, highlighting,
superscript, subscript, sizes, headings, indents, spacing,
centred, right-aligned or justified paragraphs, and the numbers and
bullets of lists are shown.
The text is as wide as on the document's page, less its margins
and any gutter at the side (A4 if it does not say; each section at
its own page's width, the page as wide as the widest; in Draft a
section in columns at its first column's), centred in the window;
scroll sideways if the window is narrower. Line
heights come from each font's own measurements, as in Word
(Calibri about 1.22 times its size, Arial 1.15; fonts not on the
disc 1.25). A document opens in Print layout: its pages one below
the other, each a sheet of its paper (View, below), so a new or
short document shows a whole page; a click in the empty part below
the text puts the caret at the end.
Info on the window's menu says what is kept but not shown
(pictures, fields, links...), the paragraphs (those in tables
too) and tables ("8 paragraphs, 1 table"), and the fonts used.
Tables are drawn: borders, shading, widths, merged cells, nested
tables, the lists and pictures in cells and the look of their table
style (scroll sideways to see a table wider than the page; a table
with tracked changes, content controls round its rows or other
things not understood is kept exactly as it was and shown as a grey
box). The caret goes into cells; you can type, select and format in
them (Tables, below), and make and change tables (Insert > Table...
and the Table menu); PNG, JPEG and GIF pictures are drawn
(Pictures, below), other drawings as a grey box with a label, links
as blue text.
Spelling marks and bookmarks are not shown at all. A field shows
its result (a page number is worked out for each page as you look,
a date or time is the one when the window was opened or a field was
last put in or updated there), or its code with View > Field codes.
Other fonts are shown in that font if the computer has it, else in
Liberation Sans.

Click in the text to put the caret there; drag to select (drag past
the window's edge and it scrolls); double-click selects a word and
a third click the paragraph; SHIFT-click or ADJUST-click extends the
selection. The arrow keys move the caret (with CTRL by words and
paragraphs), Home and End (Copy) to the start and end of the line,
CTRL-Home and CTRL-End to the start and end of the document, Page
Up and Page Down by a window, CTRL-Page Down and CTRL-Page Up to the
first line of the next or previous page (in Print layout; in Draft
they are Page Down and Page Up; a browser may keep these two keys:
use Next page and Previous page on the Edit menu); hold SHIFT to
select as it moves.
CTRL-A selects everything and Escape ends a selection. MENU never moves
the selection. The caret goes into a table's cells: click in one, or
move with the arrows; a drag or SHIFT-click from one cell to another
selects cells. A grey-box table is one thing: the caret goes before or
after it, and a click selects it (Delete removes it).
Right-to-left text (Arabic, Hebrew) is not handled yet: the caret goes
through it in stored order. CTRL-C copies the selection, CTRL-X cuts it
and CTRL-V pastes (Cmd on a Mac; also Cut, Copy and Paste on the Edit
menu), within !Letters with everything kept (a table, some rows of it or
a rectangle of its cells comes as a table, with its pictures; its
bookmarks stay with the original), and to and
from other programs with the text and its formatting (in another
document links become text and a table comes as a table, with its
table style, pictures come with their picture files; whole list items,
selected up to the
start of the next paragraph, bring their list along as a new list there,
and whole paragraphs their borders, shading and tab stops; a paragraph
whose end is not selected takes the formatting of the one it is pasted
into; a table from another program comes as a table (as text with tabs
when it is over 63 columns, 1000 rows, 8 deep or 63,000 cells); a table
you copy goes to other programs as a web page table and as text with
tabs; a PNG, JPEG
or GIF picture file with no text is pasted as a picture, other files
cannot be pasted; a picture file dropped on the window goes in where it
is dropped).

CTRL-F (or F4, or Find... on the Edit menu) opens the Find box;
CTRL-H (Replace...) opens it with the caret in Replace. Type the
text and press RETURN (Find next); Find previous looks back; at the
end it goes on from the top, and says so. Match case and Whole
words narrow the search. Replace replaces the selected match and
finds the next; Replace all replaces every one at once (one CTRL-Z
puts them back) and says how many. CTRL-G and CTRL-SHIFT-G find the
next and previous again from the document (Cmd-F, Cmd-G on a Mac).
Text in a link is found but not replaced; there are no wildcards.
Escape or Close gives the caret back to the document. The one box
works on the document last clicked in.

Typing
------
Type at the caret: any language, accents, emoji; typing over a
selection replaces it. RETURN starts a new paragraph, SHIFT-RETURN a
new line in it, TAB a tab. BACKSPACE and DELETE delete a character
(CTRL: a word); at the start or end of a paragraph they join it to
its neighbour (not at a cell's edge). Over selected cells they clear
them; BACKSPACE over whole rows removes them. INSERT switches to
overwriting (the character after the caret is shaded) and back.

CTRL-Z (or F8) undoes, CTRL-Y, CTRL-SHIFT-Z or F9 redoes (1000
steps); Undo and
Redo are also on the Edit submenu of MENU. A word typed with the
spaces after it is one step; RETURN, deleting, a pause, moving the
caret and typing over a selection each start a new one. Input
methods (for Japanese, Chinese...) show what they compose underlined
at the caret. The title shows a * while the document has changes
that are not saved; Save removes it.

Lists
-----
Bulleted and numbered lists (up to nine levels, legal numbers such
as 1.1.1, numbered headings) are shown as in Word, the numbers
worked out again as you edit. The number or bullet is not text: the
caret cannot go into it. RETURN at the end of an item makes the next
item; in an empty item it ends the list. TAB at the start of an item
(or on selected items) makes it one level deeper, to the next level
the list has; SHIFT-TAB anywhere in an item one level higher. Over a
selection with plain paragraphs, only the list items change.
BACKSPACE at the start of an item takes its number away, the text
usually staying where it was; a second BACKSPACE joins it to the
paragraph before. Format > List has Bullets > and Numbering > (the
galleries), Restart at 1, Continue numbering, Set numbering value...,
Demote, Promote and Remove from list (back to the style's indents).
The ruler and CTRL-M move an item's text and number together. A
heading numbered by its style keeps its style when its level changes
(Word may change it).

Making a list: select paragraphs and click Bullets or Numbering in
the toolbar's second row (their arrows open a gallery of six bullets
or six numberings, and None); click again to take the list away.
CTRL-SHIFT-L is the Bullets key. Typing *, - or >, or 1. 1) (1) a.
A. i. I. and the like, and a space (or TAB) at the start of a
paragraph makes it a list item; CTRL-Z at once gives back what you
typed. Format > AutoFormat lists turns that off and on. Your own
bullets and numberings, and pasting list items into another
document, are not possible yet. The guide has the details.

Formatting
----------
Select text, then press CTRL-B, CTRL-I or CTRL-U for bold, italic or
underline (again to take it off), CTRL-L, CTRL-E, CTRL-R or CTRL-J to
align the paragraph left, centre, right or justified, CTRL-SHIFT->
and CTRL-SHIFT-< for a bigger or smaller size, CTRL-= and
CTRL-SHIFT-= for subscript and superscript, CTRL-M and CTRL-SHIFT-M
to indent more or less, and CTRL-Space to clear the formatting.
SHIFT-F3 changes the case of the selection (or of the word at the
caret): capitals, then small letters, then a capital for each word, from
what the text is now (F3 alone is Save as). The
Format item on MENU has these and Strikethrough, Font, Size, Colour,
Highlight, Change case (sentence case, lowercase, UPPERCASE, Capitalize
Each Word, tOGGLE cASE) and the document's paragraph Styles (only ones
it already has), ticked to show what the selection has. Each is one
undo step. A letter whose capital or small form is longer (the German
sharp s) is left as it is.
With only a caret, CTRL-B (and the other character formats) apply to
what you type next; moving the caret, undo or RETURN forgets that.
Colour > More colours... offers Word's colours, or any colour typed
as six hex digits (RRGGBB) and RETURN; the colours listed above it
are the desktop's, and the toolbar's swatches are Word's. The text
of a link, field, tracked change or content control keeps its own
look when a selection holding it is formatted.

The toolbar across the top of the window does the same with a
click: the paragraph style, the font and the size (each with a
list from its arrow; type a font name or a size and press RETURN),
bigger and smaller, B, I, U, strikethrough, superscript and
subscript (pressed in when all the selection has it), the text
colour (shown under the A) and highlight, the four alignments,
indent less and more, and clear formatting. A field is empty, and
a button out, when the selection has several values. Clicking a
button keeps the caret and the selection where they are. Point at
a button with Help running to see what it does.

Line spacing: CTRL-1 single, CTRL-2 double, CTRL-5 one and a half
lines, and CTRL-0 puts 12 points of space above the paragraph (again:
none). Format > Line spacing, and the button at the left of the
toolbar's second row, offer 1.0 to 3.0 lines and add or remove 12
points of space before or after the paragraph. Two List Paragraphs
in a row have no space between them, as in Word.

Format > Paragraph... (or Line spacing options... on the Line spacing
menu) opens the Paragraph box: alignment, the left and right indents,
First line or Hanging, the space Before and After in points, the Line
spacing (Single, 1.5 lines, Double, At least, Exactly, Multiple, with
At: lines, or points), and the options Don't add space between
paragraphs of the same style, Widow/orphan control, Keep with next,
Keep lines together, Page break before and Suppress line numbers
(the paragraph's lines get no line number in Print layout). OK
(RETURN) applies what you changed as one undo step; Cancel or
ESCAPE changes nothing. The
four flow options decide where Print layout's pages break (in Draft,
Page break before shows a dotted line above the paragraph). Documents
are drawn with the line spacing and the space before and after that
they ask for.

The ruler under the toolbar shows the paragraph's indents, in inches
from the left margin: the top triangle is the first line, the bottom
one the hanging indent, the box under it the left indent (it moves
both), and the right triangle the right indent. Drag one: it moves
in 1/16 inch steps (SHIFT: freely) and, when let go, sets that indent
for all the selected paragraphs (one undo step). View > Ruler hides
or shows it. Margins are shown; Format > Page setup... changes
them.

The ruler shows the paragraph's tab stops too (grey: from its
style). Click SELECT on the ruler to add one of the kind shown in
the box at its left end (click the box for the next kind: left,
centre, right, decimal); drag a stop to move it, or down off the
ruler to remove it (one undo step for all selected paragraphs).
SHIFT places a stop freely. Where a stop and an indent marker meet,
press low on the ruler for the stop. A paragraph with more than 64
stops of its own, or stops kept as the file has them, beeps instead.
The small grey marks at the foot of the ruler are the default stops.

Format > Tabs... (or the Tabs... button in the Paragraph box) opens
the Tabs box: Default tab stops for the whole document, and the
paragraph's stops (the popup lists them). Type a Tab stop position,
choose its Alignment (Left, Centre, Right, Decimal or Bar) and Leader
(None, Dots, Dashes, Line) and click Set; Clear takes away the stop at
the position, Clear all every stop. OK applies it all (and the
position typed) as one undo step; Cancel or ESCAPE changes nothing.
The guide (Docs.Letters, section 6, Tab stops) explains how tabs lay
out.

Format > Borders and shading... opens a box for lines round the
selected paragraphs and a colour behind them: Setting None or Box (or
click the sides: Top, Bottom, Left, Right, Between), Style, Width,
Colour, and Fill (None, or Colour and a colour). Paragraphs next to
each other with the same borders share one box. Apply to Text puts
only the fill behind the selected words. OK is one undo step; Cancel
or ESCAPE changes nothing. Borders or shading that the file gives in
theme colours are kept as they are: changing them beeps. The guide
(Docs.Letters, section 6, Borders and shading) says how boxes are drawn.

Format painter, the brush on the toolbar's second row (also Format >
Format painter), copies formatting. Put the caret in text, or select
some, and click the brush with SELECT: the next click or drag with the
mouse paints it, and the brush lets go. With ADJUST it stays down
until you press ESCAPE (which does nothing else then) or click the
brush again. A caret picks up the character formatting there and the
paragraph's formatting; a selection inside a paragraph only the
character formatting of its first character; a selection that reaches
the end of a paragraph also the paragraph's (indents, spacing, style,
bullets and numbers). A click paints the word there, a drag the
selection, painted when you let the button go. What is painted
replaces the formatting that was there.
It is one change for Undo. The pointer does not change shape. The
guide (Docs.Letters, section 5, The format painter) has the details.

Zoom, on the window's menu, shows the page at 50% to 200% (Zoom in
and Zoom out step through them); CTRL with the mouse wheel does the
same. Each window has its own zoom; it is not saved. There is no
zoom button on the toolbar.

View, on the window's menu (after Insert), shows the document in Print
layout (pages one below the other, as they will print, with grey desk
between them: the way a document opens) or in Draft (the text on one
long page, without page edges: the quickest to type in). The next window
opens in the view you chose last, unless its file was saved in Draft
(Word's Normal view), Web layout or Outline. The view is not saved in
the file. Formatting marks shows the page and section breaks as labelled
dotted lines in Print layout (Draft always shows them). Ruler hides or
shows the ruler. Web layout shows the text as wide as the window (with
no page edges, headers or footers; the lines break again when you resize
the window); Outline shows the headings indented by their level, the
text under each one step further in, with a mark left of each paragraph
(- a heading with text under it, + one whose text is folded away, o one
with nothing under it, a small o ordinary text). In Outline SHIFT-Tab
and Tab at the start of a heading move it a level up or down, and
CTRL-SHIFT-Up and Down move a paragraph, with any text folded under it
and any field, bookmark or comment running on from it, above or below
its neighbour (never past a section break). The window
menu's Outline entry (in Outline only) has Expand, Collapse, Show level
1 to 9 or All (the headings down to that level, no ordinary text), Show
first line only, Promote, Demote, Move up and Move down; double-click
the + or - mark of a heading to fold it away or open it. The caret
never goes into folded text: Find, Replace all and Go to open the
headings over what they find, and Return at the end of a + heading
starts a new heading after its folded text. None of it is saved in the
file. Choosing a view has no key (F8 and F9 stay Undo and
Redo). Field codes (SHIFT-F9) shows each field's code, such as { PAGE },
in place of its result, until you choose it again (nothing in the
document changes). Field shading draws a light grey behind field
results: Never, When selected (the caret in the field: as it starts) or
Always; the choice is kept for next time. Edit > Update field (the field
at the caret, or those selected; in a header or footer, all of its
fields) and Update all fields put the values into the document itself
(one undo step; no keys); a locked field, or one that cannot be worked
out, is left as it is. Saving never updates a field. Insert > Page
number, Number of pages, Date and Time put in a field (in a footer, each
page shows its own number); Insert > Field... chooses any field, with a
format, or changes the code of a field selected whole. The box at the
right end of the second toolbar row says where the caret is: Page 3 of
12 (with ... after the count while the pages are still being counted,
and the section when there are several). In Print layout a click above
or below the text of a page (its margins, or the grey between pages)
puts the caret on the nearest line of the nearer page; a double-click in
the band where a page's header or footer goes edits it (Headers and
footers, below). Up and Down go from the last line of a page to the
first of the next.

Pages: in Print layout the pages break as Word breaks them: at page
breaks, Page break before and section breaks, and where a page is
full, following Widow/orphan control, Keep with next and Keep lines
together. A table carries on across pages: a row that does not fit
is split between them, header rows are drawn again at the top of
each page (a click there goes to the header row itself), and cells
merged down carry on with their rows. An Even page or Odd page break
may need a blank page: it is counted but not drawn. A section set
in columns has them side by side, filled one after another (a
column break or a New column section starts the next one; Draft
shows one column; with columns of different widths the pages after
the caret's settle a moment later); before a Continuous section
break they are balanced and the next section starts below them,
and a line is
drawn between them when the section asks for one. A section that
numbers its lines shows the numbers in the left margin (Print
layout only; a paragraph with Suppress line numbers is left out);
Format > Layout... > Line numbers... switches them on and off and
sets Start at, Count by, From text and how they restart.
The pages
follow Word's rules but often break elsewhere than Word's (the
guide's part 11 says how close); Word's are the ones that count.
Format > Page setup... sets the paper (A4, Letter, Custom...),
orientation, margins and gutter for This section, This point forward
(a section break is put at the caret) or Whole document;
Format > Layout... sets how the caret's section starts (New page,
Continuous, Even page, Odd page, New column), Different first page and
Different odd and even, the header and footer distances and the vertical
alignment; Format > Columns... sets the columns (presets One, Two,
Three, Left and Right, 1 to 45 columns, widths and spacing, Line
between) for This section, This point forward (a Continuous section
break at the caret) or Whole document. Each OK is one undo step; a
value that cannot be used beeps and the box says why.

Headers and footers: in Print layout each page shows its header and
footer (a tall one pushes the text, as in Word; Draft shows none).
Double-click one, or choose View > Header and footer, to edit it: the
body goes pale and you type, format and insert as in the text (no
page or section breaks). A page with no header can be double-clicked
too: the header is made when you first type. Escape, or a
double-click in the body, goes back to the text. In this mode the
window menu has a Header (or Footer) menu: Different first page,
Different odd and even pages, Link to previous (unticked: a copy of
its own for this section; ticked: the section's own header is
deleted without asking; CTRL-Z puts it back), Go to footer or header,
Previous and Next section, and Remove header. Typing in a header that
is 'Same as previous' changes it in every section linked to it. Undo
goes to the header or the body where the change was made. Find and
Replace in the mode look in that header only. A computed field
(page number, date) is one piece: the arrows step over it, a click
lands before or after it, and its result cannot be typed into; a
selection that takes part of a field takes the whole field. Fields
are never run or fetched from elsewhere. The guide's part 12 has
the details.

Word count, at the end of the Edit menu (it has no key), shows the
number of words, characters (with and without spaces), paragraphs and
lines of the selected text, or of the whole document when nothing is
selected, including the text in tables, links and fields (not field
codes or deleted text). A word is anything between spaces; a no-break
space or hyphen does not end it; each Chinese, Japanese or Korean
character counts as a word. Close, RETURN or ESCAPE closes it. The
guide (Docs.Letters, section 3, Word count) gives the rules.

Not yet: changing styles, typing into a document with no paragraph,
footnotes on the pages, a table of contents you can update, tracked
changes, and printing. Links are never
opened. Where Letters may differ (the guide says in each part what is
still to be checked in real Word), Word is right. On a Mac Cmd works as
CTRL for Z, Y, A, S, N, F, G, K, B, I, U, E and J (not R, L, M or H:
use CTRL-R, CTRL-L, CTRL-M and CTRL-H). Click in the window first if
characters typed with AltGr or Option are lost.

Inserting
---------
The Insert item on MENU (after Edit) has Page break (CTRL-Enter, also
on a Mac), Column break (CTRL-SHIFT-Enter outside a table), Section
break > Next page, Continuous, Even page and Odd
page, Table... (a
table; Tables, below), Tab (a tab in a table's cell), Picture... (a
PNG, JPEG or GIF picture file; Pictures, below), Symbol... (a
window of characters: click one and Insert, or double-click it),
Special character (dashes, quotes, copyright, a no-break space on
CTRL-Shift-Space, a no-break hyphen on CTRL-Shift--...),
Hyperlink... (CTRL-K: a link from the selected text to a web or
e-mail address, or to a bookmark; on a link, change or remove it;
links are never opened) and Bookmark... (CTRL-Shift-F5: give the
selected text, or the caret's place, a name; Go to and Delete a
bookmark by its name). The arrow keys never stop at a bookmark or a
spelling mark, and Backspace and Delete never delete one (an
optional hyphen, though unseen, is an ordinary character for them).
A page break is put at the caret and the paragraph is
split after it (as with RETURN); the text after it starts a new page
in Print layout, and in Draft (or with View > Formatting marks) it
shows as a dotted line labelled Page break. It is one character:
Backspace after it or Delete before it takes it out. A column break
goes in the same way; in Print layout the text after it starts the
next column of a section set in columns (the next page after the
last column, or with one column), and it shows as a dotted line
labelled Column break. No break goes in a table's cell or in a
header or footer. A section break
ends the section there (the paragraph is split, or an empty one ends
it); the section before it is a copy of the
one it was cut from, and a double dotted band labelled Section break
(Next page), (Continuous), (Even page) or (Odd page) shows under its
last paragraph (in Draft, or with Formatting marks). Delete at
the end of a section or Backspace at the start of the next one takes
the break out again. A paragraph with Page break before set shows a
dotted line above it (in Draft, or with Formatting marks). The
guide's part 8 has the details, and says more about the Symbol window
(sets, the Recently used row, which document it inserts into), the
special characters, the addresses a link accepts, the rules for
bookmark names and what deleting text does to a bookmark.

Pictures
--------
PNG, JPEG and GIF pictures are drawn at the size the document gives
them (made to fit the line, in proportion, when wider; 96 pixels to
the inch). In Print layout a floating picture is placed on its page
where the document puts it and the text flows round it (Square,
Tight, Top and bottom) or runs over or under it (Behind text, In
front of text); Format > Wrap text changes how, one undo step. Draft,
Web layout and Outline, and headers and footers in every view, show
it in its paragraph with a dashed outline. Any other picture or
drawing (EMF, WMF, SVG, BMP, a chart, a VML or shape drawing) is a grey
box with a label and is kept as it is. Click a picture to select it
(eight handles), or SHIFT-Left or SHIFT-Right over it. Drag a handle
to resize: a corner keeps the proportions (SHIFT frees them), a side
changes one side, Escape cancels, CTRL-Z undoes; in Print layout drag
a selected floating picture itself to move it. Delete or Backspace
takes a picture out (its file stays in the document). Double-click a
picture, or Format > Picture..., for the Picture box: Width and
Height in inches (0.01 to 22), Keep
proportions, Reset size (the size its image file's resolution gives:
96 to the inch when it gives none) and Alt text (clear it to remove
it); nothing out of range is accepted. Insert > Picture... asks for a
path (type it, or drop the file's icon on the box) and puts the
picture at the caret at its natural size, made to fit the text; a
picture file dropped on the window goes in where it is dropped, and a
PNG, JPEG or GIF file pasted alone (no text with it) goes in at the
caret. Over 20 MB, over 16384 pixels a side or 64 million pixels, a web
address and any other kind of file are refused, and nothing is ever
fetched. A picture copied to another document brings its picture file
(not one with a link, an SVG version or effects). Not possible yet:
text on both sides of a picture, floating pictures in headers placed
on the page, turning or cropping pictures, effects, other
kinds of picture, pictures in footnotes, and pictures in
links (shown as a grey box; Insert > Hyperlink on a picture is refused).
The guide's part 9 has the details.

Saving
------
Click MENU over the window. Save writes the document back to its file.
Save as opens a Save box: drag the icon to a directory display, or click
OK (the box is filled in with a full pathname, for a new document a name
no file has yet: OK needs a full pathname); the window takes the new
name. OK onto a file that is already there asks first: Replace or Cancel
(Return and Escape are Cancel). Dragging the icon replaces a file of the
same name without asking, as on the rest of the desktop; there is no
backup and no automatic saving. A document is file type &A7E;
'Report/docx' is 'Report.docx' on a HostFS drive, and a plain 'Report'
becomes 'Report,a7e' there (the file reaches the host a moment after
Save: if the host refuses it, the HostFS Filer says so). Save a copy
saves a copy the same way (it asks before replacing any file, even the
document's own) and leaves the window as it was (its name and its *).
What is saved holds everything the original did, including the parts
that are not shown yet (drawings, footnotes, comments...). A
locked file, a disc that cannot be written to or the file of another
open document is not saved over: Letters says why. Revert loads the
document again from its file (it asks first if there are changes; shaded
for a new document).

Keys in a document's window, as in Edit and Draw (shown on the
menus): F2 or CTRL-N a new document, CTRL-S Save, F3 the Save as box
by the caret (for a new document, Save), CTRL-F2 Close, CTRL-F10 to
the back of the windows. A key held down acts once. F4 is Find.

Info shows what the document holds; Close (or the close icon)
closes its window: with changes that are not saved it asks Save,
Discard or Cancel first (Return is Save, Escape Cancel). Quit, in
the icon bar menu, stops Letters: if documents have changes that are
not saved it asks once, Discard or Cancel (Return is Discard,
Escape Cancel); so does quitting from the Task Manager, shutting
down or leaving the desktop (Cancel stops the shutdown; after
Discard, leaving the desktop goes on to the shutdown, as with
Edit).

If a file cannot be read (it is not a .docx, or it is damaged) Letters
