The program is in this application directory: its files are
JavaScript (file type JSScript) and can be read in !Edit. The fonts
it uses to lay out Word documents are in Fonts, with their licences.
Parts it shares with other applications are in WimpLib, the
library in $.!Boot.Resources.!WimpLib.

Tables: click in a cell to put the caret there. The arrows move from
cell to cell; TAB goes to the next cell and selects its text (in the
last cell it adds a row), SHIFT-TAB to the previous one (CTRL-TAB
types a tab, but a browser may keep it for its own tabs);
CTRL-SHIFT-RETURN splits the table at the caret's row (outside a
table it puts in a column break). A drag from
one cell to another, or SHIFT with a click or the arrows, selects
cells. A click just inside a
cell's left edge selects the cell, just left of a row the row, just
above a column the column. Typing, RETURN, BACKSPACE and DELETE over
selected cells clear them; BACKSPACE over whole rows removes the rows.
The empty paragraph between two tables is never deleted (Word would
join the tables), and a table deleted whole keeps the bookmarks that
only end (or start) in it: that end goes where the table was.
Formatting, lists, links, bookmarks, symbols, pictures, Find and
Replace and the word count work in cells; a page or section break
cannot be put in one. A picture put in a cell is made no wider than
the cell. A cell always ends with a paragraph, as Word needs (a table
whose cell ends with another table stays a grey box).
Insert > Table... makes a table (columns, rows, Auto or Fixed column
width: Auto fills the cell, or the column of a page set in columns;
a Fixed width outside 0.1 to 22 inches is refused). The Table menu
(after Format) inserts rows above and below and columns to the left
and right, deletes rows, columns or the table,
selects a cell, row, column or the table, merges cells, splits cells
(a box: columns and rows), splits the table in two (CTRL-SHIFT-RETURN
too), makes rows or columns even, and types a tab in a cell (Tab, for
a caret or text in one cell: the same as CTRL-TAB, which a browser
may keep), and converts (Convert > Text to table...: the selected
paragraphs, columns and separator (paragraphs, tabs, commas or one
other character) chosen in a box; Table to text...: the table at the
caret, with a separator, its text in the table's order; a field such
as a table of contents is never cut into cells: refused). A row
added below a cell
merged down to the last row (TAB in the last cell too) gets a cell of
its own; merging keeps bookmarks in order. Every item is one undo
step; all but Insert table... and Text to table... are shaded outside a
table.
Paste a table at the caret in text and it goes in as a table (an empty
paragraph keeps two tables apart, also two pasted together and after a
deletion between two tables); in a cell it is nested (past 8 deep it
is text); over selected cells it overwrites them cell for cell,
bookmarks kept in order. A content control in a cell is left out when
a table goes into another document.
Table > Table properties... sets the table's width, alignment and
indent, the rows' height (Auto, At least, Exactly), the columns' and
cells' width and where the text sits in a cell; Table > Cell
alignment > Top, Centre, Bottom does the last at once. Format >
Borders and shading... offers Apply to Cell and Table in a table
(lines between and round cells, fill; a line taken off is taken off
the cells beside it too, not off a wider one). A table whose width
is a percentage shows Table width empty: choose Auto or Length.
Drag a line between two cells with SELECT to change the columns
beside it, the table's right edge to change its width, a row's
bottom line to change its height (not under 0.01 inch; ESCAPE
cancels; one undo step). The ruler shows the cell's text
area. See $.Docs.Letters, part 10.
Table limits: 63 columns, 1000 rows in the Insert table box, tables
eight deep, widths 0.02 to 22 inches. In Print layout rows split
between pages and header rows repeat (Pages, above). A floating
table is placed on its page in Print layout, with the text flowing
round it; type in it and use the Table menu as in any table, and drag
its edges (it cannot be moved); the other views show it in the text
with a dashed outline. Not possible
yet: moving a floating table, tracked changes to tables,
editing table styles, AutoFit, sorting and formulas (part 10 has the
list). A table with tracked
changes, content controls round its rows or cells, or a cell ending
with a table is kept and shown as a grey box.

The guide to Letters is $.Docs.Letters (part 3 is about typing,
deleting, undo and the word count, part 4 copy, paste and find, part 5
formatting (changing case, the format painter), part 6 line spacing,
space around paragraphs, tab stops, borders and shading, part 7 lists,
part 8 page and section breaks, symbols, special characters,
hyperlinks and bookmarks (the Insert menu), part 9 pictures, part 10
tables, part 11 pages, Page setup and Layout, part 12 headers, footers
and fields, part 13 columns, line numbers and text wrap, part 14
saving, part 15 new documents, closing and quitting).
